## Voice and style
- Audience: <e.g., "informed non-specialists" or "domain experts">
- Tone: <e.g., "skeptical and concrete," "warm and exploratory">
- Length norms: <e.g., "blog posts run 1500–2500 words; sections under 400 words">
- Citation style: <e.g., "parenthetical (Author Year), full bibliography at end">
- **Voice samples:** representative writing samples live in {{VOICE SAMPLES FOLDER — point this at wherever this project keeps them}}. The writer must read 2–3 genre-matched samples before drafting. The editor must do a dedicated voice pass after the critic loop closes, again referencing samples in the matching genre.
- **Canonical writing reference:** `/writing-guide/` holds the durable, evolving guidance — [`tone-of-voice.md`](writing-guide/tone-of-voice.md) (how {{harness:param.authorName}} writes), [`ai-tells.md`](writing-guide/ai-tells.md) (the screen that removes AI giveaways), and [`bilingual-publishing.md`](writing-guide/bilingual-publishing.md) (recording a finished post in two languages). Read it before drafting or reviewing any {{harness:param.authorName}}-facing text; run the AI-tells screen as a final pass before anything ships.

## Words and patterns to avoid
- Full list lives in [`/writing-guide/ai-tells.md`](writing-guide/ai-tells.md). Headlines: **em-dashes** (the #1 tell — comma/period/parens instead; softer in Danish), rule-of-three, "it's not just X, it's Y", signposting ("Importantly,", "Ultimately,"), corporate diction (delve, leverage, robust, seamless, unlock, foster, navigate), empty intensifiers (game-changing, cutting-edge), the summarising outro.
- No headers shorter than two words.
- No bullet lists where prose would do.
